* T-9: the metasystem transition over a recursive object program.
*
* Same interpreter as `metasystem-fuse.ref`, extended with a repetition node.
* The object program below is
*
*   Times(3, Seq(Lit 'a' (End), In))
*
* whose counter is GROUND while its input is unknown. Driving therefore has
* everything it needs to unwind the interpreter's own recursion: it walks the
* counter to the bottom, and what comes out is the object program with the
* loop already unrolled -- three copies of the body, and no interpreter left.
*
* This is the case that shows supercompilation is not merely inlining. The
* interpreter's recursion is structural and data-dependent; the residual has
* collapsed it into straight-line Refal, which is a new level of control
* rather than a rearrangement of the old one (Turchin 1980 4.6, 5.5).
*
* The distinction that makes this work is Turchin's own: a configuration that
* recurs on the path being expanded is a cycle and must be folded, whereas a
* configuration that recurs *after* completing is separate work with the same
* answer and must be reused. Confusing the two stops the unrolling at the
* second turn and leaves the loop residual.
*
* Object language (Turchin 1980 1.3):
*   (End)                 contribute nothing more
*   (In)                  contribute the rest of the input
*   (Lit c (P))           contribute the symbol c, then run P
*   (Seq (P) (Q))         run P, then run Q
*   (Times (Count) (P))   run P once for each mark in Count

$ENTRY Go {
  e.In = <Run (Times ('*' '*' '*') (Seq (Lit 'a' (End)) (In))) e.In>;
}

Run {
  (End) e.In = ;
  (In) e.In = e.In;
  (Lit s.C t.P) e.In = s.C <Run t.P e.In>;
  (Seq t.P t.Q) e.In = <Run t.P e.In> <Run t.Q e.In>;
  (Times t.Count t.P) e.In = <Times t.Count t.P e.In>;
}

Times {
  () t.P e.In = ;
  ('*' e.Rest) t.P e.In = <Run t.P e.In> <Times (e.Rest) t.P e.In>;
}
